    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Rigveda is the oldest known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedic-sanskrit">Vedic Sanskrit</a> text, a collection of 1,028 hymns in ten books, mostly to gods such as Agni, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a> and Soma, and most scholars place its bulk between c. 1500 and 1000 BCE.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> tradition classes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> as shruti, &quot;what is heard&quot;; scholars study the text as the work of human poets of many clans, and the Rigveda's own hymns name poets rather than claim a divine author.</li><li>It passed from teacher to pupil by memory with great fidelity, and its oldest surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, date only to c. 1040 CE.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatapatha-brahmana">Shatapatha Brahmana</a> gives the number of syllables in the Rigveda as 432,000, while the metrical text of van Nooten and Holland (1994) counts 395,563.</p></aside>
    </section>

